00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:13.500
If you’ve ever made something, a song, painting,
story, or, the pinnacle of artistic expression,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.859
a YouTube video, you know it’s incredibly
fun, and, well, pretty strange,

00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:22.869
because creativity is kind-of a mysterious
black box.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:26.720
Some days, you’re flooded with ideas faster
than you can write them down,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.380
other times your brain just feels empty.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:35.220
It can feel less like a skill and more like
the weather, coming and going, only somewhat

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.220
predictably.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.020
which, kinda… sucks, How do you ever get
better?

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:45.780
You want a recipe, a guide, just some indication
you’re on the path to eventual improvement.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.030
So, what do you do?

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.650
Well, if you want to improve your golf swing,
you ask an experienced golfer.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:58.079
Find the best musician, artist, actor, writer
and ask: To what do you attribute your success?

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:04.070
The problem is being good at something doesn’t
make you aware of why, or good at explaining

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:05.070
it.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.520
These are two separate skills.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.260
We’ve all had that brilliant professor who
just can’t teach.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.950
So, they very often have no idea.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.109
Worse, they’re absolutely convinced they
do.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.670
People love a good story.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:24.049
They’ll turn a string of lucky circumstances
into an exciting drama about overcoming challenges,

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:28.490
wrap it in a neat little bow, maybe sell it
as an expensive dead tree.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.900
Famous people are, well, famous for giving
advice like “Just be yourself”, or “Follow

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.479
your dreams and good things will come”.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:38.700
Very profound, and, totally meaningless.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.009
There aren’t many Bo Burnhams out there:
“don’t take advice from people like me

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:44.200
who have gotten very lucky.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.200
We’re very biased.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.060
You know, like Taylor Swift telling you to
follow your dreams is like a lottery winner

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.030
telling you, ‘Liquidize your assets, buy
Powerball tickets, it works!”

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:59.149
Famous actors and CEOs are weird outliers,
Their stories get the most attention… and

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:01.039
they’re the least useful.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:03.060
So, how do you get better?

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.429
We need a way of understanding creativity,

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:11.990
Not how to sketch landscapes, or write plot
twists, but how to get better at, well, getting

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.000
better

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.380
This is Ira Glass. You might recognize his voice:

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:27.170
He’s host of the incredibly popular radio
show This American Life, creators of Serial

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.170
and S-Town.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.850
If there’s such thing as a Blockbuster Podcast,
these are them.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.130
And, a while back, he gave a talk at my school.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:39.450
But what surprised me is how much of what
he said applies to making… anything.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.980
He’s been doing radio for 30 years, but
it doesn’t matter if you’re writing, drawing,

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.480
acting, or editing, creativity is largely
the same.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.530
"All of us who do creative work … we get
into it because we have good taste.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.610
But it’s like there’s a gap

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.320
what you’re making isn’t so good.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.520
But your taste — your taste is still killer,

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.010
and your taste is good enough that you can
tell that what you’re making is kind of

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.540
a disappointment to you."

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.540
If this is the process of getting better,
The good news is that everyone starts here,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.620
days, weeks, months go by with little progress,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:11.640
Want proof?

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.210
Go back and watch a YouTube channel’s first
videos, just, please not mine.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.640
But progress is exponential, As you improve,
you also get better at getting better.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.350
It just takes time and practice, ideally some
instruction.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:30.820
In other words, take more photos, make more
videos, and you’ll eventually get better.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.540
But if that’s really all it takes, why do
so few people get past this phase?

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:41.310
Well, because “just keep making things”
may be true, but it’s not really great advice,

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.190
at least not by itself.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:47.850
You might get better, or, you might make the
same mistakes over and over again,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.790
Month after month, year after year, nothing
will happen.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.160
Because there is one necessary ingredient
- taste, awareness, the desire to make better

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.950
stuff and the knowledge that you aren’t
there yet.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.410
The gap is the difference between what you
can make and what you wish you could make.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.530
You might say - but isn’t awareness of your
skill, itself a skill?

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.150
Unfortunately, yes.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.170
And that’s why it’s so easy to repeat
the same mistakes, you don’t know there

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:15.380
is a mistake.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.900
When the gap is too small, your skill too
close to your expectations, you won’t improve,

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.200
When it’s too big, you’ll give up.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.980
Only a tiny goldilocks zone can sustain creativity
long enough to get you to the exponential

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.440
curve of improvement.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:35.870
And, early on, satisfaction with your work
is entirely artificial - you’re smiling

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:41.370
at garbage - but that’s how you get past
the necessary, mundane phase of gaining momentum.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.110
But there is one problem with this theory,
if improvement is exponential, why doesn’t

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.490
it always last?

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:52.520
If this were true, people and companies would
eventually gain so much momentum they couldn’t

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:53.520
be stopped.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.600
And yet they clearly come and go.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.100
and that’s because the gap naturally shrinks
over time, you get better at judging your

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.710
own work, and you get comfortable.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:07.389
This is the plateau of progress, The reason
even the biggest companies in the world have

00:05:07.389 --> 00:05:09.380
to stay on guard,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.730
Companies like Google and Apple operate with
remarkable efficiency,

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:16.500
they’re so big it’s hard to imagine them
ever failing.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.139
But that efficiency is also their biggest
threat - as they gain momentum, it’s also

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:23.910
harder to turn, harder to adapt.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:29.169
Even if, way back in 2000, you predicted the
rise of streaming video and the fall of movie

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:34.080
rentals, You might still bet on Blockbuster
- Sure, their business will change, but they

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.660
can adapt with far more resources and efficiency
than a brand new company.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.880
So, what happened?

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.220
They hit a plateau of progress, they stopped
innovating.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.410
Management was just too satisfied,

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:51.900
When Blockbuster was way over here, Netflix
was barely here, and now, well… yeah.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.200
Which proves the power of a well-positioned
gap of motivated dissatisfaction,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:01.980
and that it’s never too late for a newcomer
to learn quickly and rise exponentially.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.910
Even on YouTube, there’s so much room for
new creators.

